<h1>why do i get ghosted after doing take-home assignments for jobs</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">You get ghosted because take-home assignments are cheap labor disguised as vetting, where companies collect your unpaid work and then hire internally, freeze the role, or never had budget to begin with. The fix is not better follow-up: it is refusing to do free work without proof the role is real and your effort gets scored.</p>

<div class="tldr">
<ul>
<li>Resume use fell to 67% in 2025, and employers increasingly use assignments to filter cheaply, but many never review them <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">(TestGorilla, 2025)</a></li>
<li>53% of employers claim they dropped degree requirements, yet the degree reset mostly stalled at announcements: only 31% followed through with real change, and fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected that change <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">(Burning Glass Institute + Harvard Business School, 2024)</a></li>
<li>Ghosting after assignments is common because companies treat candidate time as infinite and costless</li>
<li>An assignment is a test of your compliance, not your competence, and the real filter is whether you will work for free without asking questions</li>
<li>Companies that require assignments without a paid trial or a scored rubric signal a broken process where your work likely goes unread</li>
<li>You cannot follow up your way into a job that never existed, so stop treating silence as a negotiation and start treating it as a closed door</li>
</ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>The assignment economy: free work, no feedback</h2>
  <p>Take-home assignments exploded because they cost the company nothing and shift all labor to you. You research, you build, you write the strategy, you design the mockup. Then you wait. The silence is not a scheduling conflict. It is the system working exactly as designed.</p>
  <p>The 2025 TestGorilla report shows resume use dropped to 67%, down from 73% the year before. Employers needed a new filter, and "homework" was cheaper than structured interviews or paid trials. But cheap for them is expensive for you: hours of unpaid labor with no contract, no timeline, and often no human on the other end who will actually read what you built.</p>
  <p>The deeper problem is that many assignments are not vetting at all. They are extraction. A company with a frozen headcount still posts the role. A team that already has an internal candidate still collects five "comparison" portfolios. A startup that raised no new round still asks for a full growth strategy "to see your thinking." Your thinking is the product; the job is the bait.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Why the silence is structural, not personal</h2>
  <p>The skills-based hiring movement pretended to fix this. 53% of employers claim they dropped degree requirements. The Burning Glass Institute found the reality: fewer than 1 in 700 hires actually reflected that change. Most employers still hire through pedigree and internal referrals, and the assignment is a theater of meritocracy performed before the real decision gets made behind closed doors.</p>
  <p>This means your beautifully crafted take-home was often dead before you opened the brief. The hiring manager already knew who they wanted. The assignment was compliance paperwork for HR, or free consulting for a team that needed ideas, or a way to pad a candidate pool to justify an internal promotion.</p>
  <p>The ghosting is not rude. It is the logical endpoint of a process that never valued your time. When candidate labor is free and unlimited, there is no incentive to close the loop.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>The compliance test nobody tells you about</h2>
  <p>Here is the brutal truth most career advice avoids: the assignment is often not testing your skill. It is testing whether you will work for free without setting boundaries. The candidate who asks "what is the scoring rubric," "who reviews this," "is this paid," and "what is the timeline for feedback" is filtered out as "not a culture fit." The candidate who silently grinds through 20 hours of unpaid work is filtered in as compliant.</p>
  <p>This pattern appears across industries that normalized "design exercises," "case studies," and "trial projects" with no contract, no compensation, and no guarantee of human review. The filter is not competence. It is willingness to absorb cost that the company refuses to bear.</p>
  <p>The fix is not better follow-up emails. The fix is refusing to play.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How to protect yourself</h2>
  <p>Before you touch an assignment, demand proof the role is real and your work will be scored. Ask directly: who evaluates this, against what criteria, and by when. If they cannot answer, the role is not serious. If they bristle at the question, you have learned everything about their culture you need to know.</p>
  <p>Second, never submit work that could be used without hiring you. Strip proprietary detail. Watermark deliverables. Submit at a fidelity that proves your thinking without handing over executable strategy. If they need more, they can pay for it.</p>
  <p>Third, track your time and treat it as a real cost. Ten hours at minimum wage is a real number. Multiply by the number of assignments you have already done. You have paid tuition to a system that does not enroll you and issues no credential.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What actually gets you hired</h2>
  <p>The alternative is not "network more" or "optimize your LinkedIn." It is building proof of work that exists outside any single company's process, scored against a bar that is transparent and professional. A portfolio of real submissions with real feedback, where the scoring criteria are visible before you begin and the outcome is portable across employers.</p>
  <p>This is the opposite of the take-home trap. In the trap, you do the work blind, send it into a void, and hope. In a scored proof system, you know the standard, you see your result, and recruiters see it too. The work is yours. The proof is yours. The match is based on demonstrated competence, not on whether you were willing to spend a weekend building a campaign for a company that never intended to hire you.</p>
  <p>zero builds exactly this: company-shaped scenarios where you submit real work, get per-submission feedback against a professional bar, and build a scored profile that recruiters pay to access. The platform is built to eliminate ghosting, unpaid labor, and compliance theater. The work that actually wants you.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <h3>Is it ever worth doing a take-home assignment?</h3>
  <p>Only if you have verified the role is real, the timeline is specific, and the evaluation criteria are shared in writing. If the company cannot name who reviews your work and by when, you are not in a hiring process. You are in a data collection process.</p>
  <h3>How do I ask about compensation for an assignment without seeming difficult?</h3>
  <p>Ask it as a process question, not a demand: "What is the evaluation rubric and timeline for feedback?" If they have no rubric, there is no fair evaluation. If they have no timeline, there is no process. Their answer tells you whether this is vetting or extraction.</p>
  <h3>What if I need the job and cannot afford to push back?</h3>
  <p>Push back anyway, but do it with leverage. Submit at 70% fidelity with a clear note that you are happy to expand once the role and timeline are confirmed. Protect your best work. A company that disappears after a full submission would have disappeared after a partial one too, and you keep the hours.</p>
  <h3>Does a scored portfolio really replace take-home assignments?</h3>
  <p>For employers who actually hire on skill, yes. Recruiters using skills-based hiring want evidence they can compare across candidates, not bespoke projects they must evaluate from scratch. A scored, portable profile is more useful to them than your one-off homework. The employers who reject scored proof in favor of unpaid custom work are telling you exactly how they value labor.</p>
  <h3>How do I explain gaps while I build proof instead of doing assignments?</h3>
  <p>Do not explain gaps. Explain progress. "I am building a scored portfolio in [domain] with feedback from industry reviewers" is a stronger sentence than "I completed seven take-home assignments with no response." The first signals competence and initiative. The second signals you have already been filtered out seven times.</p>
</section>

